      "writtenOn": "2026-10-02T17:41:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For context Alexis, I\u0027ve been working a style check skill that we can run and reports where we differ from Skia\u0027s style (eventually can be part of CQ and/or presubmit checks).\n\nBut I wonder want the best way to write that Skill is. Like to test things out I have a small skill md file that has some of these style rules in it. Though I\u0027m trying to keep it minimally verbose to be more efficient for Agents. But then we also have our doc style.md here. In theory I can have my skill just read this md file. But are all the extra words and examples to make it human friendly worse for AI? But I also don\u0027t want to duplicate all the rules and keep both files in sync.",
      "revId": "c6eaf75cd00fea7793f27cb985275df1a9d5f229",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a92c96ab_7f674f69",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 4623515
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-02T18:23:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think, from what I know about agentic workflows, we could specify all of our style intentions here in styled.md and then have a separate \"skill.md\" that says \"you are an agent meant to ensure this cl follows skia style guides, please refer to styled.md\"\n\nThis way, the agent has access to our rules for styling and there is only one source of truth for the style guide. Also, in my experience, making a doc human readable is a great way to make it AI readable, since the models are trained on analyzing human created input. It also provides a great context workflow for agents such that they read a rule followed by an example in code.\n\nI think in general though, I\u0027ve found more success with using traditional style tools (e.g. clang format, python scripts in our CQ/CI), than I have with AI for style checking. I have been using AI to help update our .clang-format file and update our python scripts \n\n[[Maintenance] Update formatter to match modern Skia](https://skia-review.git.corp.google.com/c/1380297)\n[[presubmit] Enforce alphabetical forward declarations](https://skia-review.git.corp.google.com/c/1378896)\n\n\n\nAs I understand it, styling choices are more about keeping internal consistency rather than making perfect choices. So as long as we make a decision about how to style things and stick to it, it will work out.",
      "parentUuid": "2fbf7227_402c65fe",
      "revId": "c6eaf75cd00fea7793f27cb985275df1a9d5f229",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
